    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The five planets closest to Earth can sometimes be seen with the naked eye. In fact, humans have been staring up at Mercury"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "",
            "zh": "水星"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", Venus"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "",
            "zh": "金星"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", Mars, Jupiter"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "",
            "zh": "木星"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", and Saturn"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "",
            "zh": "土星"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " for thousands of years."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The ancient Romans paid close attention to Earth’s neighbours. They noticed that each planet had a different "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "characteristic",
            "ref": "v-characteristic"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". They even mapped the planets’ movements. That’s how the Romans gave the planets their names."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The Romans named the planets after the gods from their "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "mythology",
            "ref": "v-mythology"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Mercury, the closest planet to the Sun, has the shortest period of "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "revolution",
            "ref": "v-revolution"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Since it appeared to move faster than the others, the Romans named it after the god of messengers."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Venus shines the brightest in the night sky. That’s why the Romans gave it the name of their goddess of love and beauty. Mars was named after the Roman god of war for its red colour."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Jupiter is the largest planet in the solar system. Therefore, the Romans gave it the name of the king of their gods. They then named its neighbouring "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "ringed",
            "ref": "v-ringed"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " planet Saturn after Jupiter’s father."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The practice of naming "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "celestial",
            "ref": "v-celestial"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " objects after mythological stories caught on. When William Herschel discovered Uranus"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "",
            "zh": "天王星"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " in 1781, he wanted to name it after the English king, George III. However, others decided to call it Uranus. The name came from Ouranos, the Greek god of the sky."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Decades later, Johann Galle discovered the eighth planet. When they saw the planet’s blue "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "tint",
            "ref": "v-tint"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", the astronomers named it Neptune"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "",
            "zh": "海王星"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " after the Roman god of the sea."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p8",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "How about our Earth? You won’t find any Greek or Roman gods with that name. “Earth” actually came from Old English and "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Germanic",
            "zh": "日耳曼语的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " words, meaning “ground”. The name is pretty fitting, right?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
